/-
Sanctions/Foundations/CrossOrderComparison.lean

**Comparing two maps across an order, without asking either of them to be
monotone.**

The classical way to order the orbits of two maps is the *majorant* pattern:
dominate `g₁` by `g₂` pointwise, ask `g₂` to be increasing, and induct. Amann
(1976, §7) is the reference form — `ĝ` is a majorant of `g` when `g ≤ ĝ`
pointwise, and every majorant fixed-point theorem there requires the majorant to
be increasing.

That pattern asks for more than the induction uses. Stepping from `x` to `y` with
`x ≤ y`, the induction needs one thing: `g₁ x ≤ g₂ y`. Pointwise domination plus
monotonicity is one way to get it, and this file names the property itself:

    CrossOrderOn g₁ g₂ B A  :=  ∀ x ∈ B, ∀ y ∈ A, x ≤ y → g₁ x ≤ g₂ y

Two facts about it. It is implied by the classical pair
(`crossOrderOn_of_le_of_monotoneOn`), so nothing standing on the old hypotheses
is lost. And it is *exactly* domination by the greatest monotone minorant of
`g₂` (`crossOrderOn_iff_le_monotoneFloorOn`), which locates the weakening: the
old hypothesis is the special case in which that minorant is `g₂` itself.

**No novelty is claimed for any of this.** The order-theoretic step is
elementary, and the literature note
`PaperRefer/MathTheory/MonotoneMapsReview-HirschSmith-2005.md` records what a
search of the discrete monotone-dynamics literature did and did not turn up:
Hirsch and Smith's review is a single-map theory and contains no two-map
comparison at all, so this is a relaxation of Amann's majorant pattern rather
than a variant of a named comparison principle. The project's contribution sits
at the equilibrium level, where the maps are closed loops generated by optimizing
players.

Rule 19: general order theory, stated over bare functions and sets of reals. No
`Params`, no game content, nothing about sanctions.

## Generality, witnessed

`CrossOrderOn` is stated over arbitrary preorders.
`Examples/Order/IntegerChain.lean` inhabits it on an integer chain, at a pair
where the dominated function is *not* monotone on the upper set --- so the
instance is outside the reach of `crossOrderOn_of_le_of_monotoneOn` and shows
the majorant pattern is sufficient rather than necessary.

The greatest-monotone-minorant construction below stays over `ℝ`: it is an
infimum and needs a conditionally complete lattice, so there is nothing to
generalize it to without adding structure back.
-/
import Mathlib.Order.Bounds.Basic
import Mathlib.Order.ConditionallyCompleteLattice.Basic
import Mathlib.Tactic

namespace Sanctions
namespace CrossOrder

open Set

/-! ### Carrier types

`CrossOrderOn` and its two sufficient conditions are ordinal: they mention only
`≤` and transitivity, so they are stated over arbitrary preorders.

The greatest-monotone-minorant construction below is **not** generalized. It is
an infimum, so it needs a conditionally complete lattice and a `BddBelow`
hypothesis to mean anything, and over a bare preorder there is no `sInf` to take.
Keeping it over `ℝ` is the honest boundary: the relation is order theory, the
floor that characterizes it is lattice theory. -/

variable {α β : Type*}

/-- **Cross-order domination.** `g₁` on `B` never reaches as high as `g₂` does at
any point weakly above it in `A`.

The asymmetry in the two sets is the point of use: `B` is where the lower object
is known to live — a region a trajectory has in fact stayed inside — while `A` is
the ambient feasible set the upper object ranges over. `B` is not required to be
a subset of `A`, an interval, closed, or invariant. -/
def CrossOrderOn [Preorder α] [Preorder β] (g₁ g₂ : α → β) (B A : Set α) : Prop :=
  ∀ x ∈ B, ∀ y ∈ A, x ≤ y → g₁ x ≤ g₂ y

/-- **The classical majorant pattern implies it.**

Dominate `g₁` by `g₂` on `B`, ask `g₂` to be monotone on `A`, and cross-order
follows: `g₁ x ≤ g₂ x ≤ g₂ y`. This is the arrow that keeps every existing
consumer working — anything discharging the pointwise-plus-monotone pair
discharges the cross-order condition. -/
theorem crossOrderOn_of_le_of_monotoneOn [Preorder α] [Preorder β]
    {g₁ g₂ : α → β} {B A : Set α}
    (hBA : B ⊆ A) (hle : ∀ x ∈ B, g₁ x ≤ g₂ x) (hmono : MonotoneOn g₂ A) :
    CrossOrderOn g₁ g₂ B A :=
  fun x hx _y hy hxy => (hle x hx).trans (hmono (hBA hx) hy hxy)

/-- **Anything monotone squeezed between them will do.**

The intermediate object never has to be exhibited in the applications, but it is
what the condition is really about: cross-order holds exactly when some monotone
function sits above `g₁` on `B` and below `g₂` on `A`. This direction is the easy
one; `crossOrderOn_iff_le_monotoneFloorOn` supplies the converse by constructing
the largest such function. -/
theorem crossOrderOn_of_monotone_interpolant [Preorder α] [Preorder β]
    {g₁ g₂ h : α → β} {B A : Set α}
    (hBA : B ⊆ A) (hmono : MonotoneOn h A)
    (hlow : ∀ x ∈ B, g₁ x ≤ h x) (hhigh : ∀ y ∈ A, h y ≤ g₂ y) :
    CrossOrderOn g₁ g₂ B A :=
  fun x hx y hy hxy =>
    ((hlow x hx).trans (hmono (hBA hx) hy hxy)).trans (hhigh y hy)

/-! ## The greatest monotone minorant

`monotoneFloorOn g A x` is the infimum of `g` over the part of `A` weakly above
`x`. Lowering `x` admits more competitors, so the infimum falls: the floor is
monotone by construction. It is below `g` on `A` because `x` is its own
competitor, and it is the *greatest* function with both properties.

**Generality (2026-08-28).** The infimum is taken in the *codomain*, so that is
the only side needing order-completeness: the domain forms an upper section and
needs nothing but a preorder. The family below is therefore stated at
`[Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]`, which is exactly the structure
in which a nonempty bounded set has an `sInf`.

No linear-order refinement is required on the codomain. `csInf_le`, `le_csInf`
and `csInf_le_csInf` — the three lemmas these proofs use — all hold at
`ConditionallyCompleteLattice`, so the `ConditionallyCompleteLinearOrder`
fallback that one might expect to need is not needed here. `ℤ` carries the
instance, and `Examples/Order/` exercises the family off `ℝ` on both sides. -/

/-- The infimum of `g` over the points of `A` weakly above `x`. -/
noncomputable def monotoneFloorOn [Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]
    (g : α → β) (A : Set α) (x : α) : β :=
  sInf (g '' {y | y ∈ A ∧ x ≤ y})

theorem monotoneFloorOn_nonempty_image [Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]
    {g : α → β} {A : Set α} {x : α} (hx : x ∈ A) : (g '' {y | y ∈ A ∧ x ≤ y}).Nonempty :=
  ⟨g x, ⟨x, ⟨hx, le_rfl⟩, rfl⟩⟩

theorem monotoneFloorOn_bddBelow [Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]
    {g : α → β} {A : Set α} {x : α} (hbdd : BddBelow (g '' A)) : BddBelow (g '' {y | y ∈ A ∧ x ≤ y}) :=
  hbdd.mono (Set.image_mono (fun _ hy => hy.1))

/-- **The floor lies below `g` on `A`.** -/
theorem monotoneFloorOn_le [Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]
    {g : α → β} {A : Set α} {x : α} (hbdd : BddBelow (g '' A)) (hx : x ∈ A) :
    monotoneFloorOn g A x ≤ g x :=
  csInf_le (monotoneFloorOn_bddBelow hbdd) ⟨x, ⟨hx, le_rfl⟩, rfl⟩

/-- **The floor is monotone.** Raising `x` discards competitors, so the infimum
cannot fall. Monotone on all of `ℝ`, not merely on `A`. -/
theorem monotoneFloorOn_monotoneOn [Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]
    {g : α → β} {A : Set α} (hbdd : BddBelow (g '' A)) : MonotoneOn (monotoneFloorOn g A) A := by
  intro x _ x' hx' hxx'
  refine csInf_le_csInf (monotoneFloorOn_bddBelow hbdd)
    (monotoneFloorOn_nonempty_image hx') ?_
  rintro _ ⟨y, ⟨hyA, hy⟩, rfl⟩
  exact ⟨y, ⟨hyA, hxx'.trans hy⟩, rfl⟩

/-- **The floor is the greatest monotone minorant**, in the form the
characterization needs: any value dominated by `g` everywhere weakly above `x` is
dominated by the floor at `x`. -/
theorem le_monotoneFloorOn [Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]
    {g : α → β} {A : Set α} {x : α} {c : β} (hx : x ∈ A)
    (h : ∀ y ∈ A, x ≤ y → c ≤ g y) :
    c ≤ monotoneFloorOn g A x := by
  refine le_csInf (monotoneFloorOn_nonempty_image hx) ?_
  rintro _ ⟨y, ⟨hyA, hy⟩, rfl⟩
  exact h y hyA hy

/-- **Cross-order is exactly domination by the greatest monotone minorant.**

This is where the weakening becomes legible. The classical hypothesis asks
`g₁ ≤ g₂` with `g₂` monotone; the exact hypothesis asks `g₁ ≤ ĝ₂`, where `ĝ₂` is
the largest monotone function below `g₂`. When `g₂` is already monotone the two
coincide, since `ĝ₂ = g₂` there. When it is not, `ĝ₂` is strictly smaller
somewhere, and the exact condition is correspondingly weaker — a rule may
oscillate freely wherever the ambient structure flattens its effect. -/
theorem crossOrderOn_iff_le_monotoneFloorOn [Preorder α]
    [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β] {g₁ g₂ : α → β} {B A : Set α}
    (hBA : B ⊆ A) (hbdd : BddBelow (g₂ '' A)) :
    CrossOrderOn g₁ g₂ B A ↔ ∀ x ∈ B, g₁ x ≤ monotoneFloorOn g₂ A x := by
  constructor
  · intro h x hx
    exact le_monotoneFloorOn (hBA hx) (fun y hy hxy => h x hx y hy hxy)
  · intro h x hx y hy hxy
    exact (h x hx).trans
      (csInf_le (monotoneFloorOn_bddBelow hbdd) ⟨y, ⟨hy, hxy⟩, rfl⟩)

/-- **A monotone upper object is the equality case.** On `A`, the floor of a
monotone function is the function itself — which is why the classical hypothesis
and the exact one agree exactly when the upper object is monotone. -/
theorem monotoneFloorOn_eq_self [Preorder α] [ConditionallyCompleteLattice β]
    {g : α → β} {A : Set α} (hbdd : BddBelow (g '' A)) (hmono : MonotoneOn g A)
    {x : α} (hx : x ∈ A) :
    monotoneFloorOn g A x = g x :=
  le_antisymm (monotoneFloorOn_le hbdd hx)
    (le_monotoneFloorOn hx (fun _ hy hxy => hmono hx hy hxy))

/-! ## The floor of an upper interval, and why it replaces a chosen constant

A recurring pattern needs one specialization: `A` is an upper interval `[lo, hi]`
and the floor is read at a point of it. Then the "weakly above `x`" filter is
`[x, hi]`, and at the left endpoint it is the whole interval — so the floor at
`lo` is just the infimum of `g` over `[lo, hi]`.

That identity is what lets a *derived* floor replace a hand-chosen constant. The
pattern being replaced is a pair of conditions on some rule `g`: a level floor
`c ≤ g x` for a constant `c` picked in advance, and an order condition `x ≤ g x`.
Take `c` to be the floor instead of a numeral and the first condition stops being
an assumption — it is true by construction — leaving only the order condition.
`floorOnIcc_le_iff_self_le` is that reduction.

The floor is also monotone **with no monotonicity assumed of `g`**
(`monotoneFloorOn_monotoneOn`), which is what makes this usable when `g` is an
argmax selector: such a rule is upper semicontinuous at best, and assuming it
monotone would be assuming away the question. -/

/-- On an upper interval, the floor at the left endpoint is the infimum over the
whole interval — the "greatest lower level" a rule attains there. -/
theorem monotoneFloorOn_Icc_left {g : ℝ → ℝ} {lo hi : ℝ} :
    monotoneFloorOn g (Set.Icc lo hi) lo = sInf (g '' Set.Icc lo hi) := by
  unfold monotoneFloorOn
  refine congrArg sInf (congrArg (Set.image g) ?_)
  ext y
  simp only [Set.mem_setOf_eq, Set.mem_Icc]
  exact ⟨fun h => h.1, fun h => ⟨h, h.1⟩⟩

/-- **The level floor is free once it is the derived floor.**

With the floor itself in the role of the level constant, the conjunction
`max x (floor x) ≤ g x` — a level floor together with the order condition —
collapses to the order condition `x ≤ g x` alone. The level half holds by
construction, from `monotoneFloorOn_le`.

This is the reduction that removes a chosen constant from a basin hypothesis. -/
theorem floorOnIcc_le_iff_self_le {g : ℝ → ℝ} {lo hi x : ℝ}
    (hbdd : BddBelow (g '' Set.Icc lo hi)) (hx : x ∈ Set.Icc lo hi) :
    max x (monotoneFloorOn g (Set.Icc lo hi) x) ≤ g x ↔ x ≤ g x :=
  ⟨fun h => le_trans (le_max_left _ _) h,
   fun h => max_le h (monotoneFloorOn_le hbdd hx)⟩

/-- A bounded rule always admits its floor: the side condition of the reduction
above is discharged by any uniform bound, which a feasibility constraint gives
for free. -/
theorem bddBelow_image_of_forall_le {g : ℝ → ℝ} {A : Set ℝ} {m : ℝ}
    (h : ∀ x ∈ A, m ≤ g x) : BddBelow (g '' A) := by
  refine ⟨m, ?_⟩
  rintro _ ⟨y, hy, rfl⟩
  exact h y hy

end CrossOrder
end Sanctions
